Both redesigned routes met or exceeded their selected human-muscle torque references at the measured angles: vastus medialis for the extensor and biceps femoris short head for the flexor (Figure~ef{fig:redesign_validation}). Both tests span \ang{-119} to \ang{5}. Extensor torque margins range from \qty{0.24}{
ewton\meter} to \qty{3.52}{
ewton\meter}; flexor margins range from \qty{0.42}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-105} to \qty{17.0}{
ewton\meter} at \ang{-14}.

The flexor identification of record is the two-bracket fit of Section~ef{sec:identification} on the four cross-validation tests: the $l_{0}=\qty{48.5}{\cm}$ and \qty{41.0}{\cm} tests in training and the $l_{0}=\qty{45.7}{\cm}$ and \qty{40.6}{\cm}-tendon tests held out. Its adopted design parameters are a length offset $\chi_{0} = \qty{+3.94}{\mm}$ and attachment stiffness parameters $\chi_{1} = \qty{39.98}{\kilo
ewton\per\meter}$ and $\chi_{2} = \qty{14.73}{\kilo
ewton\per\meter}$ (Table~ef{tab:AdoptedXi}). Figure~ef{fig:FlxPin_optimized} shows that, at these terms, the optimized model follows the measured torque across the range of motion, including the loss of torque near full extension that the rigid-body prediction does not produce. The training-set RMSE is \qty{2.165}{
ewton\meter} for the $l_{0}=\qty{48.5}{\cm}$ test and \qty{1.084}{
ewton\meter} for the $l_{0}=\qty{41.0}{\cm}$ test, and the held-out validation tests give \qty{1.368}{
ewton\meter} at \qty{45.7}{\cm} and \qty{1.611}{
ewton\meter} at \qty{40.6}{\cm} with tendon (Table~ef{tab:FlxPinGof}).
